 {:db/id "q-175312" :question/id #uuid "a6022d77-1162-5219-998d-09e9a6dcc354" :question/math-academy-id "q-175312" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x=3$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ equals $3.$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ equals $3.$\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not equal to* $3.$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\ne 3$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-175312/ma-source-field/b67191599fa2f471bb635670d4013d8c8470611d466858d4dcaadef99c31785f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
