 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "3dc836e6-a152-59c0-a817-45e5b70cbfb8"] :question/problem "$\\,$ $x\\in R$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following: \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not* a member of $R.$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin R$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-175315/ma-source-field/cde3e526a44fb09602ab0168e3ae5ab12194f62352c24fb31f14c553a02e1439"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
