 {:db/id "q-176834" :question/id #uuid "9ba92e56-0c67-534f-87e7-bf0cdfa829cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-176834" :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $4(x-3)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}=20(z-1)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2} & =20(z-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/5} & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{1/20} \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/5} & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{1/20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(3,-2,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-176834/ma-source-field/b1875be31de5f555e56fa5f0648a254bf8c7d444ceef3b76b64229cc37939d03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
