 {:db/id "q-178256" :question/id #uuid "d601c130-3a93-5630-8b35-93cbbfae4d1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-178256" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125$ have multiplicity $2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-5)-25(x-5) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-25)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-5)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x-5{)}^{2}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-178256/ma-source-field/d165d5112c3fc386f49ec34181a6ced8e689f092b16f7f28705d781ac3bcd33b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
