 {:db/id "q-178288" :question/id #uuid "1dd05574-640c-5b9b-a9a5-c23bbe5e5973" :question/math-academy-id "q-178288" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.99)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.01$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.99)^{7} & =(1+(-0.01))^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(-0.01)+21(-0.01)^{2} \\\\  & =1-7(0.01)+21(0.000\\,1) \\\\  & =1-0.07+0.002\\,1 \\\\  & =0.932\\,1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-178288/ma-source-field/83b86282e674a816d65e498f45850828a8bd56be4e149ac03f3e11befb2cc44c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
