 {:db/id "q-31633" :question/id #uuid "359dea7b-c28c-5695-9f78-2decd6212dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-31633" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨5,3⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-5,-3⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨5,3⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5t+{C}_{1},\\,3t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-5,-3⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨5(2)+{C}_{1},\\,3(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-5,-3⟩ & =⟨10+{C}_{1},\\,6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-15$ and ${C}_{2}=-9.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨5t-15,\\,3t-9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨5t-15,\\,3t-9⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t-15 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=3, \\\\ 3t-9 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=3,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-31633/ma-source-field/e408e63a529421f7965d79123a524bf98ec2c1059c1d2680fe7fbf027609ec3f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
