 {:db/id "q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07" :answer-field/id #uuid "a491e86e-1311-5936-86d4-f5c880062fc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07/17744788330f58841addf62dc48c17a05e2f80fa6fdc64b1079a2792d0799996" "q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07/97217f148d283b2a801b997676ec5e3fb6645b795768229d725ef4661661acbe" "q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07/cd52cf227650f39a8c6263ff2c716eaad063c335a52efa09f7d551199797d4f1" "q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07/577cb9c5dab6da3808833c53d87670e53309521ee74e4a5d2a38217f53e1fe66" "q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07/d30e858d75a10955b00db25dc9355af51eb20ee0e80bca7870da7cbac118565e"] :answer-field/correct "q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07/17744788330f58841addf62dc48c17a05e2f80fa6fdc64b1079a2792d0799996" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-32092" :question/id #uuid "d83ee387-80b0-5c7f-bd0c-361b0164d684" :question/math-academy-id "q-32092" :question/problem "Consider the lines $y-5x=-1$ and $kx-y=-3$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines don't intersect, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $y-5x=-1$ and $kx-y=-3$ intersect exactly at one point, then the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution  for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$  \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n *If the lines **don't** intersect, then $det[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]=0.$*" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-32092/ma-source-field/ec7c730b5d3b1533a4c2473dddd07415e6241faa7f88a32cfbd1eb27e3236a07"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
