 {:db/id "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44" :answer-field/id #uuid "12200322-ef19-5f83-936e-a28151c6b492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277"] :answer-field/correct "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-326236" :question/id #uuid "17c5a462-c29c-5b82-b891-ee8c95c1220f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326236" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
