 {:db/id "q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a417b69-1171-54da-bd75-777eee884877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0/123942cd749876c17a6b2111c522f57d2f90392a6bbce40d0548fb0c6e09c0bf" "q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0/0a09441f4183675937fd5c7d25dc74c984d4215bff154f382c148de72daff13c" "q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0/0bb036e6f9e3ade09811ac7a129d6d153604bcdb525df124b120017cec66a044" "q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0/fe8e7e9d97ee2c82ec4b022a5376bac5592f847974d7c031c043b4432cb41f51" "q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0/9a58e8520188fa184cb9883a40850d11225e6ed1205009948338b9bd5632466b"] :answer-field/correct "q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0/fe8e7e9d97ee2c82ec4b022a5376bac5592f847974d7c031c043b4432cb41f51" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-331457" :question/id #uuid "fde1c354-d593-5da3-9167-ee1bf6674333" :question/math-academy-id "q-331457" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{3t}-{e}^{-t}$ for $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{-t}} & =L{{e}^{3t}}+L{-{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+1},\\,s>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s+1)-(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1-s+3}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331457/ma-source-field/913297b3b23df07d1ca57c9a405d246b01775038a418172c48ed572211933bb0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
