 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "b57e3584-dab2-5078-9ba7-3beaafafb71c"] :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ \nSo, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39953/ma-source-field/6e895b4ab79796aa9366bb4f2f90ce9331af5b3369642f169acc46b18a963d7f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
