 {:db/id "q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040" :answer-field/id #uuid "c97e3a1a-a6d7-5da5-9304-dedf20c2e8e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040/de3b684342615eb42bd3fd791082af53f5ed14a4c85c1274c82199581db3ef5e" "q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040/410e2dc125509aee85f6bb48fa5e5495ef0e380ffb00773fbc223639c4077898" "q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040/4e0c847f7ac76b7ca11f25a5d6df01b04c62fd7268be753dc15c7f8325c318eb" "q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040/43302fc5fb707030f40212d8c4d09f51198eefbd417f1e4b1248861c1969b11b" "q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040/2f05b45e93be56ad2633f42704ce90bf93b6d876be7ee43dc16e2fe54b7d6a35"] :answer-field/correct "q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040/2f05b45e93be56ad2633f42704ce90bf93b6d876be7ee43dc16e2fe54b7d6a35" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-40175" :question/id #uuid "daa0bc39-0770-52e4-a043-5e620f02bd66" :question/math-academy-id "q-40175" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-6x+y \\\\ 12x-2y\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]y,\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-40175/ma-source-field/d7a69b0c73d07293ceda4df419b54c824fb2f958e62fd7da56d323f63abcd040"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
