 {:db/id "q-46423" :question/id #uuid "3c7b2eef-6b92-58ae-a674-05e715b64388" :question/math-academy-id "q-46423" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$, what is the highest possible degree of $P+Q?$" :question/worked-solution "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $7.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{7}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{7}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-46423/ma-source-field/ef46ba0de2236c63a4a4b8a77d3ec36cc8e0891e6dfebec0cd41861fe3d18092"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
