 {:db/id "q-47077" :question/id #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-47077" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
