 {:db/id "q-47101" :question/id #uuid "4f05bf34-eff5-5d2f-b84c-b025710dd1a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-47101" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)=(x-2)(x+2)(x-5).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $g(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[2,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4]$, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $g(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $g(2)$ and $g(4).$\n\nSince $g(2)=0$ and $g(4)=-12,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-12,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-12,0].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
