 {:db/id "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8c5ef9-0c37-5adf-839c-99af73ffe32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/e1f821bff3e32bcda7ee7d2acc9e426a5a753b7b256a5501d0ce1d20a5bda7bf" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/14bc67b7e38b2c052c4733b4bce02e6d254fda218336aa05c2e028b261f50d41" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/b93c3cf420a425b6411c3139ba44610b8e2992ebd3c4c7cc5029f7e8e17c86cb" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/874fd58cb2eca1d6694d5ba945287685db91fb08e85ff2bbb8feb4c314371bc7" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/7b01c9fabfde241ca062f40c2fbaf32a30d5a7ece0a3f326dce0777e7c35f2c7"] :answer-field/correct "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/e1f821bff3e32bcda7ee7d2acc9e426a5a753b7b256a5501d0ce1d20a5bda7bf" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-48372" :question/id #uuid "51c2bb08-d00d-5a09-b26c-2e5b5f8107fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48372" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f636c252da8e886c1f4bf9f0d87387339fce91e5a72f6275e6b24bba787d288.png)\n\nAnna is an environmentalist and is studying the impact of deforestation in a pine forest located in a square-shaped region. Anna discovers that the area of ​​the deforested region is decreasing at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month}.$ At what rate are the region's diagonals decreasing if the diagonals are $20\\text{ km}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=20.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{0.4}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.02\\,\\text{km}\\text{/month}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the region are decreasing at a rate of  $0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
