 {:db/id "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420" :answer-field/id #uuid "95508699-f6d9-5815-8c2e-e441b5ff17ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6"] :answer-field/correct "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-49027" :question/id #uuid "d7fa8c48-a61b-5fbf-8ae6-af7bb5265a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49027" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1+x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\cdot (1+x)-\\sqrt{x}}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)-2x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}{(1+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{2}}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1-2}{2\\sqrt{2}(1+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{18\\sqrt{2}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
